      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town grew around a massive sawmill on White Oak Point, where logs floated down the Mississippi River to be processed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zemple%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A roundhouse on the former Minneapolis and Rainy River Railway once housed 12 engines, marking Zemple's early rail importance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zemple%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lumber dominated Zemple's economy, with a planing mill, veneer mill, and box mill that later burned down in later years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zemple%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The original landowner, Zempel, owned most of the area and was elected the first village president, giving the town its name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zemple%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Zempels farm sat on the south end of the village, anchoring the community's early agricultural life.",
